      <div class="modal-body">
            <h6 class="fw-bold">Overview</h6>
            <p class="text-justify">The Naoero Medical Centre (NMC), formerly the Republic of Nauru Hospital (RON Hospital), is Nauru&rsquo;s only hospital and the top of the in-country health system. It is in Denigomodu District. It was formed in 1999 by merging the government-run Nauru General Hospital with the Nauru Phosphate Corporation Hospital. In 2025 the Government signed a 10-year management agreement with UAE-based Global Mission Support Services (GMSS), and the facility was relaunched as the Naoero Medical Centre. The 2025 Act expressly allows this kind of arrangement: Cabinet may engage a private entity to provide or manage health and medical services, subject to conditions protecting public servants and returning government assets in good condition (Section 125).</p>
            <p class="text-justify"><strong>Note:</strong> The Schedule to the Act still names the facility &ldquo;RON Hospital,&rdquo; while the operator now uses the name Naoero Medical Centre. Treat the two names as the same facility, and search both when doing desk research. The NMC remains a government-funded &ldquo;health and medical service facility&rdquo; under Section 4 even though a private company manages it. Classify it as &ldquo;National Hospital &mdash; public (government-funded, privately managed under Section 125).&rdquo; Bed counts differ between sources: a 2010 Ministry of Health strategic plan described a 56-bed hospital, and current secondary sources report 65 beds.</p>
            <h6 class="fw-bold">Role</h6>
            <ul>
                <li>Serve as the only hospital on the island, providing emergency, inpatient and outpatient care for the whole population</li>
                <li>Receive referrals from private health practitioners and providers (Section 65); private practitioners may not refer patients directly overseas without going through a health and medical service facility and the Committee (Section 65(3))</li>
                <li>Recommend overseas medical referrals to the Overseas Medical Referral Compliance Committee where additional expertise or a differently resourced facility is needed (Sections 64 and 66)</li>
                <li>May be used for medical tourism services approved by the Minister, provided this does not reduce services to Nauruans (Sections 111&ndash;114).</li>
            </ul>
            <h6 class="fw-bold">Clinical Service</h6>
            <p><strong>Core Capability Areas</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Emergency medicine, intensive and critical care, general and laparoscopic surgery, pediatrics, women&rsquo;s health and maternity, mental health, ophthalmology, dialysis, physiotherapy, dental care, pharmacy and telemedicine (NMC service list)</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Diagnostic &amp; Support Infrastructure</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Laboratory, radiology including CT, x-ray and ultrasound; hemodialysis unit with vascular access surgery</li>
                <li>Reported activity, December 2025 to May 2026: 22,279 outpatient visits, 3,687 emergency visits, 680 admissions, 405+ surgeries and 119 births (NMC website)</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Typical Bed Capacity</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>About 65 beds, including a 10-bed emergency department and a 14-bed acute care unit (secondary sources); 56 beds in 2010</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-justify"><em><strong>Note:</strong> Legal basis: Health and Medical Services Act 2025, Sections 4, 5, 64&ndash;73, 111&ndash;114 and 125, and the Schedule. Management agreement, bed count and history come from secondary sources (Wikipedia, citing a Ministerial Statement on Health of 16 April 2026); services and activity figures come from the NMC website (nmc.gov.nr).</em></p>
